1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,365
Germanicus saved the man

2
00:00:01,405 --> 00:00:03,388
he later accused of killing him.

3
00:00:03,599 --> 00:00:04,952
According to Tacitus,

4
00:00:04,992 --> 00:00:06,229
a storm drove Piso

5
00:00:06,269 --> 00:00:07,820
towards a rocky coast.

6
00:00:07,860 --> 00:00:09,979
Germanicus sent warships to help.

7
00:00:10,019 --> 00:00:11,941
He was the emperor's adopted son,

8
00:00:11,981 --> 00:00:13,602
heading east to settle disputes

9
00:00:13,642 --> 00:00:16,612
among Rome's provinces and allied kingdoms.

10
00:00:17,008 --> 00:00:19,186
Piso, appointed governor of Syria,

11
00:00:19,226 --> 00:00:21,148
was supposed to serve under him.

12
00:00:21,188 --> 00:00:23,412
Instead, he gave soldiers gifts,

13
00:00:23,452 --> 00:00:25,037
replaced strict officers,

14
00:00:25,077 --> 00:00:26,163
and ignored orders

15
00:00:26,203 --> 00:00:28,094
to send troops to Armenia.

16
00:00:28,607 --> 00:00:30,961
The emperor's son held the higher command.

17
00:00:31,265 --> 00:00:32,224
Piso was building

18
00:00:32,264 --> 00:00:33,783
a following of his own.

19
00:00:34,179 --> 00:00:36,952
Then Germanicus fell ill near Antioch.

20
00:00:37,093 --> 00:00:39,306
Tacitus says human remains,

21
00:00:39,346 --> 00:00:41,465
spells, and lead tablets bearing

22
00:00:41,505 --> 00:00:42,696
his name were found hidden

23
00:00:42,736 --> 00:00:44,429
in the floor and walls.

24
00:00:44,640 --> 00:00:47,482
Germanicus believed Piso had poisoned him.

25
00:00:47,693 --> 00:00:48,791
Dying, he asked

26
00:00:48,831 --> 00:00:50,594
his friends to avenge him.

27
00:00:50,990 --> 00:00:52,576
After Germanicus died,

28
00:00:52,616 --> 00:00:54,588
Piso wanted Syria back.

29
00:00:54,798 --> 00:00:55,908
His own son urged

30
00:00:55,948 --> 00:00:57,243
him to return to Rome,

31
00:00:57,283 --> 00:00:58,311
warning that going back

32
00:00:58,351 --> 00:01:00,718
to Syria could mean civil war.

33
00:01:00,929 --> 00:01:02,409
Piso chose force.

34
00:01:02,449 --> 00:01:04,348
His men fought Roman troops.

35
00:01:04,388 --> 00:01:05,489
He lost.

36
00:01:05,630 --> 00:01:07,927
Now he had to face the Senate.

37
00:01:08,324 --> 00:01:10,908
Outside, crowds dragged off his statues

38
00:01:10,948 --> 00:01:12,347
to smash them and threatened

39
00:01:12,387 --> 00:01:14,460
violence if he escaped punishment.

40
00:01:14,500 --> 00:01:17,177
Inside, his accusers alleged poisoning.

41
00:01:17,217 --> 00:01:18,431
But Tacitus says they

42
00:01:18,471 --> 00:01:19,827
failed to prove it.

43
00:01:20,131 --> 00:01:21,775
He could be condemned for corrupting

44
00:01:21,815 --> 00:01:22,727
the army and taking

45
00:01:22,767 --> 00:01:24,367
up arms against Rome.

46
00:01:24,508 --> 00:01:25,397
That did not prove

47
00:01:25,437 --> 00:01:27,258
he had poisoned Germanicus.

48
00:01:27,771 --> 00:01:30,018
Before judgement, Piso was found

49
00:01:30,058 --> 00:01:31,341
dead in his room,

50
00:01:31,381 --> 00:01:32,959
a sword beside him.

51
00:01:33,471 --> 00:01:35,591
The Senate condemned him anyway,

52
00:01:35,631 --> 00:01:38,543
and ordered its decree displayed in bronze.

53
00:01:39,056 --> 00:01:41,109
Copies survive in Spain.

54
00:01:41,622 --> 00:01:43,361
They condemn his cruelty,

55
00:01:43,572 --> 00:01:45,413
his corruption of soldiers,

56
00:01:45,453 --> 00:01:47,634
and his attempt at civil war.

57
00:01:47,937 --> 00:01:50,536
They repeat Germanicus's accusation,

58
00:01:50,678 --> 00:01:52,731
but make no finding of murder.

59
00:01:52,941 --> 00:01:54,597
The man Germanicus saved

60
00:01:54,637 --> 00:01:56,423
was condemned as a rebel.

61
00:01:56,668 --> 00:01:57,638
Whether he had also

62
00:01:57,678 --> 00:01:59,737
been his killer remained unproved.

63
00:02:00,650 --> 00:02:02,804
History remembers the victors.

64
00:02:03,619 --> 00:02:05,010
We remember the rest.
